# Summary
Introduced: the patch newly lists object versions inside bucket_delete_debug_snapshot and returns those samples in the unauthenticated debug HTTP response. Existing debug endpoints were already unauthenticated/local-only, but this change broadens the disclosed data to live object keys and object-version metadata.
When ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT is enabled, requests under /__argmin/debug/ are handled before the normal S3 request/authentication path. The bucket-delete-attempt endpoint accepts only a bucket name from the path and calls storage.bucket_delete_debug_snapshot without SigV4 authentication or owner authorization. This commit adds per-object-PG object-version sampling to that snapshot and formats the sampled key and metadata into the HTTP response. As a result, any local process able to reach the debug listener can query /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/<bucket> and learn up to one object key/version sample per metadata PG for private buckets, even when it has no S3 credentials. The binary config limits this endpoint to loopback listeners and it is disabled by default, so the impact is limited, but it still exposes protected bucket-listing metadata once the diagnostic endpoint is enabled.

# Resolution
Fixed for production/release builds by the local-debug endpoint hardening commits:

- `45497afe` gates local debug route dispatch and helper code behind `cfg(any(test, feature = "local-debug-endpoints"))`, and rejects `ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=1` at startup unless the binary was compiled with that non-production support.
- `73a36bcd` makes `local-debug-endpoints` a debug/test-only feature by adding a release-build `compile_error!`, and updates UAT smoke handling so debug-hook smokes cannot be run against release or externally supplied binaries.

The bucket-delete debug endpoint can still disclose this diagnostic data in explicit debug/test builds with `local-debug-endpoints`, but release artifacts cannot include or enable the route. Production default builds reject the environment variable before startup.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the local debug endpoint is optional but handled before normal S3 authentication/authorization.
- [x] Confirm /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/<bucket> accepts only a bucket name and performs no credential or owner check.
- [x] Confirm the new snapshot code enumerates object-version metadata for the requested bucket.
- [x] Confirm the HTTP debug response serializes object keys and version metadata.
- [x] Demonstrate with a real local HTTP request lacking Authorization that private object metadata is returned; note loopback/disabled-by-default mitigation.
## Report
Validated the finding. Rubric-guided review confirmed the local debug handler is invoked before normal S3 processing/authentication at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:691-700. The bucket-delete-attempt debug route parses only the bucket name from the URL and calls storage.bucket_delete_debug_snapshot without SigV4 or owner checks at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1041-1067. The commit-added storage code scans object versions with max_keys=1 per metadata PG at crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:11775-11815 and converts StoredObject rows into samples containing key, version_id, generation_id, size, layout, and timestamps at crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:11899-11926. The HTTP body formatter emits those fields, including key={:?}, at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1240-1261. I added a targeted PoC unit test that creates a private bucket/object, enables ServeConfig.local_debug_endpoint, starts the local HTTP server with zero normal request slots, and sends a raw GET /__argmin/debug/bucket-delete-attempt/debug-leak-bucket request with no Authorization or x-amz-* signing headers. The test passed and printed a 200 OK response containing object_version_sample index=0 object_pg_id=0 kind=live key="private-object-key" version_id=null generation_id=1 size=11 layout=standard. This is concrete evidence that an unauthenticated local caller can learn live object key/version metadata when the debug endpoint is enabled. Direct crash attempt was not applicable and the PoC exited status=0 because this is an information disclosure, not a memory-safety crash. valgrind was unavailable in the container. I also ran lldb non-interactively and set a breakpoint at local_debug_response; the backtrace showed handle() calling local_debug_response at serve.rs:692 before normal request processing, with the debug path as the request path. The lldb-induced pause caused the test to timeout after continuing, but the debugger trace still confirmed the pre-auth call chain. Impact is limited by config: crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:444-460 requires ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT to use a frontend role and loopback listen address, but it does not authenticate local callers.

